and I hope you'll find that useful.

148
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:14,000
So, without any further ado,

149
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:16,480
let's get started, shall we

150
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:20,720
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

151
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:30,480
I get a fair number of emails

152
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,560
with questions about policy and procedure

153
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:33,680
and things like that,

154
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,360
but I would say they are equaled,

155
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,440
if not surpassed, by the numbers of emails

156
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:40,080
that I get with people asking my advice

157
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,800
about how to resolve some kind of problem

158
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:44,240
that they're running into

159
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,480
in their troop or their pack or their crew.

160
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:49,280
One that recently came over the wire said

161
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,320
we're experiencing some difficulty in our troop

162
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,800
and it's causing animosity between the scoutmaster

163
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:56,159
and the assistant scoutmasters

164
00:07:56,159 --> 00:07:57,520
and the committee members,

165
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:58,960
and they went on to ask my advice

166
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:00,240
about how to solve it.

167
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,000
I get emails from parents

168
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,800
whose scouts are having difficulties

169
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,400
getting along with the adults

170
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:08,240
who are volunteering for their troop.

171
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:12,400
I get emails about adults arguing with each other.

172
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:15,120
If you've spent any amount of time in scouting,

173
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,600
you know that we have difficulties and problems

174
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,000
and obstacles to overcome once in a while.

175
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:20,720
One of the things

176
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:22,640
that I think really helps with this

177
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:24,480
is a little bit of perspective.

178
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:26,400
You know this is all nothing new.

179
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:30,720
This has been going on for as long

180
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:32,320
as scouting has been around,

181
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:33,520
and before there was scouting

182
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:34,480
there were other things.

183
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:37,280
and you know we're dealing with human beings,

184
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:39,600
and human beings can be a little unpredictable

185
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:40,960
and a little temperamental,

186
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:44,320
and we have to work with each other.

187
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,800
Baden Powell wrote this about a century ago

188
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,680
when he was confronted with one of these issues.

189
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:51,520
He said: it is almost beyond belief

190
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:54,560
that grown-up or nearly grown-up adults

191
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,120
can take little matters so seriously

192
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:59,360
and so narrowly as some of them do.

193
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,680
If they only had a sense of humor

194
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:04,640
or had a slightly wider range of view

195
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:06,880
so that they could see the other side of the question

196
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,560
or its greater aim,

197
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:11,920
they too would smile at the littleness of it all.

198
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:14,320
I was bealed to the other day in a case

199
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,920
which was evidently considered

200
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:18,640
of vast importance by the contending parties,

201
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:22,240
but which would have seemed ridiculously simple

202
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:24,800
to an outsider who saw both sides

203
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:25,920
and the higher motive

204
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:28,080
which was supposed to be their joint aim.

205
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,240
My reply to them

206
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:33,680
was one which might apply to many similar cases

207
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:35,200
where the contestants

208
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:38,400
cannot at once see the right line to take,

209
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:39,280
and it was this:

210
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:41,600
It is curious to me

211
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,080
that men who profess to be good Christians

212
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:45,360
often forget,

213
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:47,360
in a difficulty of this kind,

214
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,080
to ask themselves the simple question:

215
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:51,520
what would Christ have done

216
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:52,960
under the circumstances?

217
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:54,640
and be guided accordingly.

218
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:57,040
Try it the next time you are in difficulty or doubt

219
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:58,240
as to how to proceed.

220
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:02,720
That very good advice still rings true today.

221
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,520
It's a century later. it still rings true today,

222
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:09,040
and while Baden Powell called upon people

223
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:11,120
to emulate the founder of the faith

224
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:12,400
that he followed,

225
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:15,680
I do want to make sure that everybody understands

226
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:19,120
that whether you follow Christianity as a faith

227
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:21,040
is not really what's important here,

228
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:23,040
but that we use the example

229
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:25,920
and the tenets of conflict resolution

230
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:27,440
about getting along

231
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:29,040
and assuming goodwill.

232
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:33,040
Now there are conflicts and difficulties between adults

233
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:34,640
and there's conflicts and difficulties

234
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:36,480
between adults and scouts.

235
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:37,360
For some reason,

236
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:39,600
there are a lot of adult volunteers

237
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,080
who feel they have to be hard on scouts

238
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:43,840
and they get a little overheated

239
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:45,600
or they want to prove a point

240
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:49,760
or win some actually really pointless power struggle

241
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:53,840
with a scout between the ages of 11 to 18..

242
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:54,800
When you look at it,

243
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:55,840
come on,

244
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:59,440
Really, we can work this out right

245
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:01,920
if goodwill will work it out,

246
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:05,040
If everybody just kind of cools their jets a little bit

247
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:08,080
and gains some perspective, as Baden Powell said,

248
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:09,600
having a sense of humor

249
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:12,800
or a slightly wider range of view,

250
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:14,320
a different perspective,

251
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:16,800
so that you can see the other side of the question

252
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:18,720
and it's greater aim.

253
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,880
You will probably do what I do a lot of times

254
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:22,240
when I get these emails

255
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:23,760
and that is kind of a smile

256
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:25,520
at the littleness of it all,

257
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:26,800
because in the end,

258
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:29,920
a lot of this does not amount to a hill of beans.

259
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:31,680
Let's take a step back

260
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:33,920
and give the whole thing some thought

261
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,760
and find more compassion and understanding.

262
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:39,920
All too often, when these conflicts come up,

263
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:41,280
it's the failure of people involved

264
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:43,439
to possess the compassion and understanding

265
00:11:43,439 --> 00:11:44,720
required of scouts

266
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:48,400
to transform difficulties and conflicts

267
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:50,480
into positive experiences.

268
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:53,360
Sometimes all we need to solve these monster,

269
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:55,280
what looks like monstrous problems

270
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:57,280
but are really not,

271
00:11:57,280 --> 00:11:59,600
is a slight shift in attitude.

272
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:02,319
What would things look like

273
00:12:02,319 --> 00:12:04,400
if our first assumption

274
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:06,720
when confronted with any one of these conflicts

275
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:07,520
or difficulties

276
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:09,520
was the assumption of goodwill?

277
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,360
The patrol leader who forgot to do something

278
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:12,959
may have honestly forgotten to do it,

279
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:16,800
not just willfully ignored their responsibility.

280
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:18,800
I forget all kinds of things

281
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:20,160
I don't know about you,

282
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:21,839
so we assume goodwill,

283
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:24,160
The scout who earned

284
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:25,760
three merit badges last month.

285
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:26,560
you know what?

286
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:28,560
they may have worked hard to achieve something

287
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:30,480
rather than trying just to get around

288
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:31,360
your standards.

289
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:33,600
I would assume goodwill.

290
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:36,560
an honest misunderstanding between

291
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:38,400
adults, or an honest misunderstanding

292
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:39,680
between scouts,

293
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:41,280
or an honest misunderstanding

294
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:42,959
between scouts and adults.

295
00:12:42,959 --> 00:12:44,400
it's not a punishable crime.

296
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:47,199
I would assume goodwill

297
00:12:47,199 --> 00:12:48,720
if you have to explain something

298
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:50,720
to someone for the thousandth time

299
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:53,280
and who hasn't had to do that.

300
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:53,680
I know

301
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:54,959
you know what.

302
00:12:54,959 --> 00:12:56,000
it might just be

303
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,160
that we're not communicating well,

304
00:12:58,160 --> 00:12:59,280
rather than

305
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:00,560
they're failing to listen.

306
00:13:01,199 --> 00:13:02,640
I would assume goodwill

307
00:13:03,439 --> 00:13:06,240
if it looks like someone's pulling in the other direction.

308
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:07,680
look for good intentions

309
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:09,040
in what they're doing

310
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:10,480
before you start pulling back.

311
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:12,959
I would assume goodwill

312
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:15,520
that the scout sitting across from you

313
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:16,880
at the scout master's conference.

314
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:18,880
they're doing their best,

315
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:21,439
so assume goodwill on their part.

316
00:13:21,439 --> 00:13:22,800
the scout sitting across from you

317
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:25,040
at the border review is doing their best.

318
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:27,280
assume goodwill on their part.

319
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:28,640
an eagle candidate

320
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:30,000
is conducting his project

321
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:31,680
and finding his way through the process

322
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:33,200
and the best way that he knows how.

323
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:35,280
assume goodwill.

324
00:13:35,280 --> 00:13:36,800
our assumption of goodwill

325
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:37,680
is really the key

326
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:41,360
because it starts us from the perspective

327
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:43,520
of that. other people aren't necessarily wrong.

328
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:46,800
we just may misunderstand what they're up to.

329
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:49,280
let me read you another quote

330
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:50,400
from Bate and Powell.

331
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:50,800
he says:

332
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:52,240
brothers we are to our boys,

333
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:53,520
brothers to each other,

334
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:54,400
we must be

335
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:56,400
if we're going to do any good.

336
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,960
aloofness or jealousy could not exist

337
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:01,280
where there is a true ideal of brotherhood.

338
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:02,960
what we need,

339
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:06,080
and what, thank God, we've got in most places in our movement,

340
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:08,560
is not merely the spirit of good nature,

341
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:09,600
tolerance,

342
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:11,760
but of watchful sympathy and readiness

343
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:12,880
to help one another.

344
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:14,400
we don't only need this,

345
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:15,440
but we've got to have it

346
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:17,680
if we're going to teach our boys

347
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:19,360
by the only sound way,

348
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:21,360
that is, through our example,

349
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,200
that the greatest principles

350
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:26,800
are goodwill and cooperation.

351
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:28,880
so if you're listening to this podcast,

352
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:31,840
it's more than likely that you are an adult volunteer

353
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:32,880
in scouting,

354
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:34,560
that you have some responsibilities

355
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:35,840
and things to take care of

356
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:38,480
and that on occasion you feel unappreciated.

357
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:43,120
I think that feeling has more to do a lot of times

358
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:46,080
with conflicts and difficulties that arise

359
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:47,520
than anything else.

360
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:49,200
so let me tell you about my buddy, Burke.

361
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:52,160
I don't think Burke really cared too much

362
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:53,840
about what other people had to say

363
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:56,240
about the ceremonial campfires he built

364
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:57,360
for our scout camp,

365
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:00,240
and they are some really impressive campfires

366
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:01,600
we're talking about,

367
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:05,280
you know, a graduated pyramid of campfire

368
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:06,880
that stands about six feet tall-

369
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:08,000
just beautiful things.

370
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:10,000
what he cared about most

371
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,960
was not what everybody else said about them

372
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:14,880
but what he had to think of them himself.

373
00:15:15,760 --> 00:15:17,280
now I can recall some occasions

374
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:18,880
where, at least in my eye,

375
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:21,760
engaging the reaction of the people who were watching the campfire.

376
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:24,080
the campfire performed perfectly.

377
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:25,120
they lit easily,

378
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:27,920
they flooded the ceremonial campfire circle with light.

379
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:30,640
they died down at the appropriate time in the program,

380
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:32,400
like they were on a timer almost.

381
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:34,640
but to Burke's eye

382
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:36,720
there may have been some slight imperfection,

383
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:38,160
something it improved upon.

384
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:40,560
is he always immune to what people had to say about them?

385
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:41,680
well, I don't think so.

386
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:46,560
I don't think any of us are immune to what we hear people say about what we do.

387
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:49,360
but his own standard was the important benchmark,

388
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:52,480
not his expectation about how others would react to his work

389
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:55,440
because, seriously, he could have put a lot less effort

390
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:57,600
into building those campfires

391
00:15:57,600 --> 00:15:58,640
than he did

392
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:00,720
and people probably wouldn't have noticed.

393
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:03,760
but it was important to him to get it right.

394
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:07,280
our expectations for acceptance of

395
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,280
an engagement in our work

396
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,080
as volunteers can have unintended results.

397
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:12,880
I don't know about you,

398
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:15,520
but I know I get disappointed and discouraged

399
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:18,960
when people's reactions don't meet my expectations.

400
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:23,840
we can get subtly or not so subtly aggressive or resentful,

401
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:27,520
and this colors the work that we do with our scouts or our fellow leaders.

402
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:31,840
now if I'm disappointed in the reaction that my work as a scouter receives,

403
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:35,600
I'm really tempted just to kind of tell everybody off

404
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:37,840
and take my ball and bat and go home.

405
00:16:37,840 --> 00:16:40,400
but you know that's not the thing to do right

406
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:43,439
now. if we didn't attach a personal value

407
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:45,680
to the acceptance of our work

408
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:48,240
but only to the work itself,

409
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:49,520
how would that change our approach?

410
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:54,480
if we didn't allow our personal expectations to get in way of the program,

411
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:55,600
what would happen?

412
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:59,680
now? I'm not suggesting that you lower the expectations you have for yourself.

413
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:04,960
I'm saying: don't have expectations for the way people are going to react.

414
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:07,040
now that sounds a little impossible

415
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:08,480
and maybe even a little heretical,

416
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,360
but give it some thought.

417
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:11,440
if we keep working hard

418
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:13,920
and we don't tie that work to an expectation

419
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:15,840
about how people are going to react to it,

420
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:17,840
that means that we'll be working hard

421
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:20,000
and accepting whatever we get in return.

422
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:23,600
as a veteran of a dozen or so years of staffing summer camp,

423
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:26,320
I know that some troops can show up

424
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:28,800
and they can really make something of their experience,

425
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:31,280
and some seem to be pulling in the opposite direction,

426
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:32,320
no matter what you do,

427
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:34,160
and that's just plain frustrating.

428
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:36,080
once you run into that sort of frustration,

429
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:37,760
there are two basic ways to react.

430
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:40,560
you can change about how you do things

431
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:43,679
to produce the result that they expect.

432
00:17:43,679 --> 00:17:45,840
in other words, you can alter the program

433
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:47,520
and you can change everything around

434
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:51,440
and try and please people who are really difficult to please.

435
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:54,000
chasing that result, chasing their reaction:

436
00:17:54,639 --> 00:17:57,040
most of the time it leads to shortcuts,

437
00:17:57,600 --> 00:18:00,000
but it gets results and it fulfills expectations,

438
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,560
even if those expectations are pretty hollow.

439
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:04,560
the other reaction is to work harder,

440
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:07,600
because working hard is in itself a good thing to do.

441
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,919
if we adhere to an internal standard of value

442
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,600
that energizes our work,

443
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:15,120
that work is going to be all that much more valuable to others.

444
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:17,120
the right kind of energetic hard work

445
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:20,960
with good intentions creates participation and engagement,

446
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:23,680
and engagement usually fulfills the expectations

447
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:25,200
of the people you're serving,

448
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:27,040
creating a positive feedback loop

449
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:28,880
that energizes everybody.

450
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:30,640
now scoutmasters experience this.

451
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:33,200
we can work with the expectation of some result

452
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:36,320
and mistakenly think that the higher our expectations

453
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:38,560
of a result the better.

454
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:40,639
if scouts don't react as we expect,

455
00:18:40,639 --> 00:18:44,879
well, we get frustrated because we're aimed at their reaction.

456
00:18:44,879 --> 00:18:47,280
when we meet with these frustrations and disappointments,

457
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:49,679
we ought to be able to double down on our efforts

458
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:54,080
and stop expecting things and start doing things.

459
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:57,520
we have to be brave and resolute about these things.

460
00:18:57,520 --> 00:19:00,000
examine your own expectations

461
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,040
and understand the value of the work itself.

462
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:05,120
if your work doesn't advance the aims of scouting,

463
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:07,440
well, stop doing it that way.

464
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:08,560
I mean nothing personal,

465
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:10,480
but we all miss things sometimes.

466
00:19:10,480 --> 00:19:13,920
and double down on work that advances the aim of scouting,

467
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:17,360
I can guarantee your energy level will rise

468
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:19,760
and you'll begin to experience better results.

469
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:24,400
this is podcast number. let's see 234.

470
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:27,440
that's 234 weekly podcasts.

471
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:29,120
that's about four years,

472
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:30,320
and there was a point

473
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:32,480
where recording a weekly podcast

474
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:35,280
brought very little in the way of results,

475
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:37,600
bought very little in the way of reactions,

476
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:40,320
and I really treasured the very few that I got

477
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:44,960
because it let me know that this was actually useful to some people.

478
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:47,840
now, if I had gone on for 50 or 60 podcasts

479
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:49,680
and nobody reacted positively,

480
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:51,360
I probably would have given up,

481
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:54,399
because at that point I would have understood

482
00:19:54,399 --> 00:19:57,280
that what I was doing really didn't have any value to people.

483
00:19:57,280 --> 00:19:59,920
and sometimes it's a big challenge

484
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:02,000
to turn on the computer and turn on the microphone

485
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,760
and record a podcast every week,

486
00:20:03,760 --> 00:20:05,360
and there are there are periods of time

487
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:07,600
where I don't get a whole lot of the way of reaction,

488
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:08,720
but I keep doing it

489
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:11,600
and this isn't because I'm an especially great person.

490
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:14,400
okay, I'm pretty convinced that this helps people.

491
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:16,720
I'm going to keep doing it until I get it right.

492
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:18,879
234 podcasts later,

493
00:20:18,879 --> 00:20:22,160
I like to think that this helps people more often than not.

494
00:20:22,160 --> 00:20:23,920
the work that you're going to do,

495
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:25,440
you're going to put it out there,

496
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:26,960
and the reason that you're going to do it

497
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:29,680
is because it's the right thing to do,

498
00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:33,520
not because you're hoping that people will react well to it.

499
00:20:33,600 --> 00:20:36,720
and if you don't get the kind of reaction that you expect,

500
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,240
don't blame the customer.

501
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:39,440
look at yourself,

502
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:40,639
look at what you're doing

503
00:20:40,639 --> 00:20:42,879
and you might have to make some adjustments to it,

504
00:20:42,879 --> 00:20:46,320
but usually it's just putting a little more hard work into it

505
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:48,160
that gets the reaction.

506
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:50,879
so, bringing this back around full circle,

507
00:20:50,879 --> 00:20:54,000
we started by talking about how to resolve conflicts

508
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:57,200
and how to deal with problems between adult volunteers

509
00:20:57,200 --> 00:20:59,360
or adult volunteers and scouts,

510
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:00,639
and the way to do. it

511
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:03,920
is all about perspective and attitude

512
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:05,760
and having a sense of humor.

513
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:07,920
the source of a lot of our difficulties is

514
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:10,320
is we expect everybody to react

515
00:21:10,320 --> 00:21:11,920
to the work that we do in a certain way

516
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:12,880
and when they don't,

517
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:14,080
we get disappointment,

518
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:17,680
and disappointment leads to a lot of negative emotions,

519
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:19,280
and we kind of get in that spiral

520
00:21:20,080 --> 00:21:22,480
to avoid conflicts and difficulties.

521
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:23,680
a lot of times,

522
00:21:23,680 --> 00:21:26,640
the thing that we need to do is to concentrate

523
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:29,440
on doing good work that advances the aims of scouting

524
00:21:30,640 --> 00:21:51,440
music.

525
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:52,960
well, thanks for listening to this

526
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:56,000
234th edition of the scoutmaster podcast.

527
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:57,600
here's how you can keep in touch.

